//? Title: Birkhoff Interpolation: Values, Derivatives and Uniqueness
//? Subtitle: A hand-solvable cubic, nonpoised families and numerical scaling limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given scattered values and derivatives of a function, find a polynomial that
satisfies all the conditions. Missing derivative orders are allowed, but the
conditions may be redundant, inconsistent or nearly dependent. We derive the
linear system, recover the original cubic from values and curvatures, and
exhibit both infinitely many solutions and a family that becomes singular.
Datamunge exposes the constraint matrix and derivative evaluator, and its
scaled solver reports numerical failure separately from a successful fit.
)

\toc

> The method

For N constraints, seek \(p(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}c_kx^k\), of degree **at most**
\(N-1\). A condition \(p^{(d_i)}(x_i)=v_i\) becomes row i of

$$
A c=v,\qquad A_{ik}=\begin{cases}
\dfrac{k!}{(k-d_i)!}x_i^{k-d_i},&k\ge d_i,\\0,&k<d_i.
\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(Each matrix row evaluates a specified derivative of every monomial. Columns below that derivative order are zero.)

Derivative orders need not be consecutive at a node. This is the lacunary
feature of Birkhoff interpolation: unlike Hermite data, a value and a second
derivative need not come with a first derivative.

In exact arithmetic, the fixed-node problem is poised when A is nonsingular:
every right-hand side then has one unique coefficient vector. A singular A may
have no solution or infinitely many for a particular v. A numerical solver also
has a third practical limit: coefficients may be extremely sensitive to small
changes even when the exact matrix is nonsingular.

```{cpp, file="figures/birkhoff_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  diagram.node("left",{0,1.6},"At x = -1\nvalue and curvature")
         .node("right",{0,-1.6},"At x = 1\nvalue and curvature")
         .node("system",{6,0},"Four linear equations\nA c = v")
         .node("answer",{12,0},"Unique cubic\nc = (1, 2, 3, 4)")
         .edge("left","system").edge("right","system")
         .edge("system","answer","full rank")
         .text({6,-3},"No first derivatives were prescribed.");
  diagram.save_svg("build/birkhoff_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/birkhoff_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original example's two values and two curvatures determine four coefficients, despite the missing first-derivative data.)
\alttext(Value and curvature measurements at minus one and plus one feed a four-equation linear system, yielding cubic coefficients one, two, three and four.)

> Simple example: recover the original cubic

The conditions are \(p(-1)=-2\), \(p''(-1)=-18\), \(p(1)=10\), and
\(p''(1)=30\). Since \(p''(x)=2c_2+6c_3x\), adding and subtracting the
curvature equations gives \(c_2=3\), \(c_3=4\). The value equations then
give \(c_0=1\), \(c_1=2\). Thus \(p(x)=1+2x+3x^2+4x^3\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::BirkhoffConstraint> conditions{
      {-1,0,-2},{-1,2,-18},{1,0,10},{1,2,30}};
  const auto matrix=dm::algorithms::birkhoff_matrix(conditions);
  const auto c=dm::algorithms::birkhoff_polynomial(conditions);
  if (c.empty()) return 1;
  std::print("| node | order | c0 factor | c1 factor | c2 factor | c3 factor | target | fitted |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<conditions.size();++i) {
    const auto& condition=conditions[i];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.10f} |\n",condition.node,condition.order,
        matrix[i][0],matrix[i][1],matrix[i][2],matrix[i][3],condition.value,
        dm::algorithms::birkhoff_eval_derivative(c,condition.node,condition.order));
  }
  std::print("\nRecovered coefficients: {:.8f}, {:.8f}, {:.8f}, {:.8f}.\n",c[0],c[1],c[2],c[3]);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| node | order | c0 factor | c1 factor | c2 factor | c3 factor | target | fitted |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| -1 | 0 | 1 | -1 | 1 | -1 | -2 | -2.0000000000 |
| -1 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 2 | -6 | -18 | -18.0000000000 |
| 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 10 | 10.0000000000 |
| 1 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 2 | 6 | 30 | 30.0000000000 |

Recovered coefficients: 1.00000000, 2.00000000, 3.00000000, 4.00000000.
// result_end
\caption(The matrix and fitted derivative values are returned by the core API. A curvature constraint is checked against the second derivative, not plotted as if it were a function value.)

```{cpp, file="figures/birkhoff_cubic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto c=dm::algorithms::birkhoff_polynomial({{-1,0,-2},{-1,2,-18},{1,0,10},{1,2,30}});
  if (c.empty()) return 1;
  std::vector<double> x,value,curvature;
  for (int i=0;i<=150;++i) {
    const double point=-1.5+3.*i/150;
    x.push_back(point);
    value.push_back(dm::algorithms::birkhoff_eval(c,point));
    curvature.push_back(dm::algorithms::birkhoff_eval_derivative(c,point,2));
  }
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines(x,value,"p(x)",dm::RGB{124,58,237},2)
   .points({-1,1},{-2,10},"values",dm::RGB{220,38,38},7)
   .title("Recovered cubic").x_label("x").y_label("p(x)");
  auto d=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  d.lines(x,curvature,"p second derivative",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
   .points({-1,1},{-18,30},"curvatures",dm::RGB{220,38,38},7)
   .title("Prescribed curvatures").x_label("x").y_label("second derivative");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(p).add(d).size(1100,470).fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/birkhoff_cubic.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/birkhoff_cubic.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original curve is retained, with function values and derivative observations shown on separate axes. Extrapolation beyond the nodes is displayed, without a general approximation guarantee.)
\alttext(The left panel shows the cubic with constrained values at minus one and plus one. The right panel shows its linear second derivative passing through curvatures minus eighteen and thirty.)

> A nonpoised problem has more than one possible outcome

For a quadratic, prescribe \(p(-1)=p(1)=0\) and \(p'(0)=0\).
Every \(p_\lambda(x)=\lambda(x^2-1)\) satisfies these conditions, so there is
no unique interpolant. Changing only the derivative target to 1 makes the same
matrix inconsistent: the endpoint conditions force \(c_1=0\), whereas
\(p'(0)=1\) demands \(c_1=1\). The API returns an empty vector in both cases;
an empty result does not distinguish these two mathematical situations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/birkhoff_nonunique.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| midpoint derivative target | numerical fit returned |\n| ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (double target:{0.,1.})
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",target,
        !dm::algorithms::birkhoff_polynomial({{-1,0,0},{1,0,0},{0,1,target}}).empty());
  std::vector<double> x;
  for (int i=0;i<=100;++i) x.push_back(-1.2+2.4*i/100);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colors{{37,99,235},{148,163,184},{220,38,38}};
  int color=0;
  for (double lambda:{-1.,0.,1.}) {
    std::vector<double> y;
    for (double point:x) y.push_back(dm::algorithms::birkhoff_eval({-lambda,0,lambda},point));
    plot.lines(x,y,std::format("lambda = {}",lambda),colors[color++],2);
  }
  plot.points({-1,1},{0,0},"endpoint values",dm::RGB{17,24,39},6)
      .title("Three members of an infinite solution family")
      .x_label("x").y_label("p(x)").y_limits(-1.3,1.6).size(850,460)
      .save_svg("build/birkhoff_nonunique.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| midpoint derivative target | numerical fit returned |
| ---: | :--- |
| 0 | false |
| 1 | false |
\image(figures/birkhoff_nonunique.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Three distinct polynomials meet the zero endpoint values and zero midpoint slope. The slope is a derivative condition, not an extra zero value at the midpoint.)
\alttext(An upward parabola, a downward parabola and the zero polynomial all pass through zero at the endpoints minus one and plus one and have zero slope at the centre.)

The original report's second rejected scheme, \(p'(0)=p''(-1)=p''(1)=0\),
is also nonpoised for degree at most two: the constant coefficient is unconstrained,
and the curvature rows are identical. Rejection is appropriate even though the
zero polynomial itself satisfies the conditions.

> Checking the theory: approach to singularity

Move the derivative node to t and prescribe \(p'(t)=1\), retaining the zero
endpoint values. The matrix and its determinant are

$$
A(t)=\begin{pmatrix}1&-1&1\\1&1&1\\0&1&2t\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\det A(t)=4t,\qquad p_t(x)=\frac{x^2-1}{2t}\quad(t\ne0).
$$
\alttext(The derivative-node matrix has determinant four times t. For nonzero t the unique polynomial is x squared minus one divided by twice t.)

The coefficient size grows as \(1/(2|t|)\), while a perturbation delta in the
derivative target changes \(c_2\) by \(\delta/(2t)\). Thus the geometry itself
amplifies measurement error as t approaches zero. A numerically returned answer
cannot make this sensitivity disappear.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/birkhoff_sensitivity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| derivative node t | determinant 4t | numerical fit | recovered c2 | exact c2 |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (double t:{.5,.1,.01,.001,1e-6,1e-12,1e-16,0.}) {
    const auto c=dm::algorithms::birkhoff_polynomial({{-1,0,0},{1,0,0},{t,1,1}});
    if (c.empty()) std::print("| {:.2e} | {:.2e} | unresolved | — | {} |\n",t,4*t,t?std::format("{:.4e}",.5/t):"none");
    else std::print("| {:.2e} | {:.2e} | returned | {:.4e} | {:.4e} |\n",t,4*t,c[2],.5/t);
  }
  std::vector<double> x;
  for (int i=0;i<=120;++i) x.push_back(-1.+2.*i/120);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colors{{37,99,235},{217,119,6},{220,38,38}};
  int color=0;
  for (double t:{.5,.1,.02}) {
    const auto c=dm::algorithms::birkhoff_polynomial({{-1,0,0},{1,0,0},{t,1,1}});
    if (c.empty()) return 1;
    std::vector<double> y;
    for (double point:x) y.push_back(dm::algorithms::birkhoff_eval(c,point));
    plot.lines(x,y,std::format("t = {}",t),colors[color++],2);
  }
  plot.title("The unique solution becomes sensitive")
      .x_label("x").y_label("p(x)").y_limits(-28,6).size(850,460)
      .save_svg("build/birkhoff_sensitivity.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| derivative node t | determinant 4t | numerical fit | recovered c2 | exact c2 |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |
| 5.00e-01 | 2.00e+00 | returned | 1.0000e+00 | 1.0000e+00 |
| 1.00e-01 | 4.00e-01 | returned | 5.0000e+00 | 5.0000e+00 |
| 1.00e-02 | 4.00e-02 | returned | 5.0000e+01 | 5.0000e+01 |
| 1.00e-03 | 4.00e-03 | returned | 5.0000e+02 | 5.0000e+02 |
| 1.00e-06 | 4.00e-06 | returned | 5.0000e+05 | 5.0000e+05 |
| 1.00e-12 | 4.00e-12 | returned | 5.0000e+11 | 5.0000e+11 |
| 1.00e-16 | 4.00e-16 | unresolved | — | 5.0000e+15 |
| 0.00e+00 | 0.00e+00 | unresolved | — | none |
\image(figures/birkhoff_sensitivity.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The formula and fitted coefficients can be compared directly. The exact matrix is nonsingular at t=1e-16, but the numerical pivot threshold cannot establish a unique fit there.)
\alttext(Three downward-shifted parabolas have the same zero endpoint values; their centres grow in magnitude as the derivative constraint moves towards zero.)

> Scaling and numerical limits

The solver equilibrates each matrix row and column before pivoted elimination.
This removes the old absolute pivot cutoff's dependence on a single coefficient's
units. For example, the valid line through \((0,1)\) and \((10^{-16},2)\)
has coefficients \((1,10^{16})\): column scaling resolves it even though the
unscaled slope column is tiny.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto c=dm::algorithms::birkhoff_polynomial({{0,0,1},{1e-16,0,2}});
  if (c.empty()) return 1;
  std::print("| intercept | slope | value at 0 | value at 1e-16 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n"
             "| {} | {:.6e} | {} | {} |\n",c[0],c[1],
             dm::algorithms::birkhoff_eval(c,0),dm::algorithms::birkhoff_eval(c,1e-16));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| intercept | slope | value at 0 | value at 1e-16 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1.000000e+16 | 1 | 2 |
// result_end
\caption(An exact affine control distinguishes a change of coordinate scale from genuine dependence among constraints.)

Scaling does not guarantee a well-conditioned monomial basis. Pivots at most
\(64N\epsilon\) on the equilibrated matrix are treated as numerically singular;
this is an implementation threshold rather than an exact rank theorem.
The routine uses \(O(N^3)\) work and \(O(N^2)\) storage. Negative orders and
nonfinite conditions are rejected, and nonrepresentable arithmetic raises an
overflow error. Empty coefficients evaluate as the zero polynomial, so callers
must check for an empty fit before interpreting it as an interpolant.

> Conclusion

The original cubic is recovered from values and second derivatives, with the
matrix and fitted derivatives visible. The quadratic controls distinguish
nonuniqueness, inconsistency, exact poisedness and numerical resolution. Scaling
fixes the tiny-coordinate affine control, while the approaching-singularity
example retains its unavoidable sensitivity. Generated polynomial controls are
sufficient here; an observed dataset would not establish these exact properties.

> References

- Birkhoff, G. D. (1906). *General Mean Value and Remainder Theorems with Applications to Mechanical Differentiation and Quadrature*. Transactions of the American Mathematical Society, 7(1), 107–136. https://www.jstor.org/stable/1986339
